From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bartosz Nitka <niteria@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 16:22:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: G16: stop the firmware for a deep sleep
 and restart it warm

The M4's GPU driver refused every sleep that powers the SoC down, so deep
sleep needed the driver blacklisted. Add the sleep path, following G17:

- .prepare drains the GPU, closes publication and leaves the stop to the
  sleep's commit, with every refusal behind it.
- The commit idles the firmware, stops it and lowers the GFX domain, on
  G17's C path, which now also takes a firmware whose control registers
  are the ASC's own.
- .complete raises the domain and restarts the firmware warm: the RTKit
  handshake, no initialization. iBoot's last wake program has reprogrammed
  the GPU's tables by then.

A refused sleep finds the firmware never stopped. Stopping it any earlier
doesn't work: after a stop in .prepare and an alarmtimer refusal, RTKit
discovery timed out and the GPU was lost until reboot. That matches G17's
"only a real wake restarts the firmware".

Differences from G17, all measured on a J713:
- The sleep clears the GFX TVM gate (0x0 after a real wake, 0x1 after a
  refusal) and genpd doesn't know, so it's enabled before the domain is
  raised.
- The PMP vote stays through the sleep. A release before the stop also
  worked, in 4 sleeps, but the commit has no polled path for one.
- There's no bridge and no power-on-done message, as on the M4 Pro.

The M4 publication host test gets the flag, and a case for a publish
during a sleep.

[Dylan: resolved onto the J604 tree, where this replaces our own G16
 sleep path (g16_deep_sleep: a stop at the apple-s2r commit, and the AGX
 release in the PMP last stop), which never ran through a real sleep:
 g16/sleep.rs is this commit's, our sleep/policy.rs and the
 asahi_g16_commit_* glue go, and so does the g16_deep_sleep parameter,
 which only that path read. boot.rs keeps our changes and takes these
 RTKit helpers in place of our raw(). The drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c
 comment is left to the sleep driver port.]

