 // SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only OR MIT
 
-//! System sleep.
+//! System sleep: the firmware goes idle, its domain goes off, and it restarts warm.
 //!
-//! Suspend-to-idle keeps the SoC powered, and the GPU firmware runs through it with its own idle
-//! power management (`asahi.g16_idle_power_off`). A sleep that powers the SoC down
-//! (`pm_suspend_via_firmware()`) takes the GPU's ASC, its power domain and the state iBoot set up
-//! for the cold start with it. G16 has no qualified firmware stop (RTKit idle, then the ASC
-//! stopped) and warm restart (the ASC started again with the firmware's state kept in DRAM, the
-//! RTKit handshake and no initialization, as macOS does on T8140), so such a sleep is refused
-//! once the firmware has been touched. A G16 that never got that far has nothing to lose; the PMP
-//! still refuses the sleep if the AGX vote was taken.
+//! The firmware isn't initialized again. It keeps its rings and state in DRAM, so a restart is
+//! the RTKit handshake. That only works after a real wake: iBoot's last wake program reprograms
+//! the GPU's tables with the domain on and leaves it off, and after a stop with no sleep the
+//! firmware doesn't answer the handshake.
 //!
-//! For bring-up, `asahi.g16_deep_sleep=1` takes such a sleep instead: the firmware is idled and
-//! its ASC stopped, and restarted warm early in the resume (`.resume_early`, after the power
-//! domain is back). Whether the G16 firmware comes back from that on the J604 is what the
-//! parameter is for; a firmware that does not come back leaves the GPU failed until reboot, the
-//! rest of the system resumes.
+//! So `.prepare` only drains and closes publication, and the stop is left to the sleep's commit,
+//! which runs it with interrupts off and every refusal behind it. A sleep refused before then
+//! finds the firmware never stopped. The order is G17's, which macOS's power words on T8132
+//! agree with. The PMP vote stays through the sleep.
 //!
-//! An idled firmware doesn't come back with its power kept: only a sleep that reaches the
-//! hardware restarts it. So where the sleep driver can take it (apple-s2r), the idle and the stop
-//! are left for the sleep's commit, past every refusal, with interrupts off (`g16/glue.c`), as
-//! for the PMP: a sleep refused before then finds the firmware still running and costs the GPU
-//! nothing. Without the sleep driver they run in `.suspend_late` (interrupts still on), and a
-//! sleep refused after that leaves the firmware stopped until one that reaches the hardware.
+//! Only a real wake restarts the firmware. A sleep refused after a stop leaves it stopped and
+//! publication closed until the next real sleep, with the domain on.
 
